<h2>The case against the applicant</h2>
<p>The prosecution's narrative was of a planned killing. On 30 July 2022, the applicant and three co-accused were said to have conspired; the next day, the deceased — Anwarul Haque, aged 22 — was lured by phone to Tukmeerpur Pulia and stabbed. The post-mortem recorded haemorrhagic shock from a single stab wound to the abdomen. The motive alleged was the applicant's wish to pursue a relationship with a woman who was involved with the deceased. The applicant was arrested in Bangalore roughly four months later, and had been in judicial custody since 16 December 2022.</p>
<p>The connecting evidence was of a particular kind: two witnesses identified the applicant from CCTV footage, and the prosecution relied on call detail records. No recovery was effected from the applicant; the weapon was allegedly recovered from a co-accused.</p>

<h2>How the bail history shaped the outcome</h2>
<p>The procedural history did much of the work in this case, and it repays attention.</p>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>2024</strong> — A bail application is withdrawn with liberty to reapply after material witnesses are examined; the Court requests that the trial be expedited.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>8 January 2026</strong> — The trial court dismisses a fresh application, principally because many public witnesses remain to be examined.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>8 October 2026</strong> — With public witnesses examined and only official witnesses left, the High Court grants bail.</div>
</div>
<p>Justice Sachin Datta's reasoning followed that arc precisely. The apprehension of witness influence, which had justified the earlier refusals, no longer applied once the public witnesses stood examined. What remained of the prosecution's case — the CCTV identification and the CDRs — raised questions of evidentiary weight that only the trial could answer. And the custody arithmetic had become stark: about three years and nine months, against an applicant with no other case, where the delay in trial was not attributable to him.</p>

<h2>Gravity alone cannot answer Article 21</h2>
<p>The State's opposition rested on the gravity of the offence, the identification evidence, the call records and the Bangalore arrest. The Court, relying on the Supreme Court's decision in <em>Arvind Dham v. Enforcement Directorate</em> (2026), held that the seriousness of an offence cannot by itself justify the refusal of bail where the right to a speedy trial under Article 21 is affected. The applicant, it concluded, deserved release on bail subject to stringent conditions.</p>

<h2>The conditions</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
<li>Personal bond of Rs 50,000 with two sureties in the like amount.</li>
<li>No contact with witnesses or with the family of the deceased.</li>
<li>Attendance on every date of hearing before the trial court.</li>
<li>No departure from the NCT of Delhi, and no travel out of India, without permission.</li>
<li>Residential addresses to be disclosed and kept updated with the Investigating Officer.</li>
<li>A working mobile phone with location services on, shared with the IO; reporting to the SHO on the first Monday of each month.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The practice point</h2>
<p>The order confirms a rhythm that Delhi practitioners will recognise in long-custody murder matters. A withdrawal with liberty, sought at the right moment, is not a defeat; it fixes the milestone — examination of material witnesses — at which the next application becomes winnable. When that milestone is crossed, the application should demonstrate three things from the record: that the tampering apprehension has been overtaken by events, that what remains of the prosecution evidence raises weight questions reserved for trial, and that the custody period now does work that gravity alone cannot answer. The Court's caveat, as always, was that nothing in the order touches the merits, which remain entirely open at trial.</p>
